region(11,278,450);table(11,503,621,465,502);region(11,629,740);region(12,60,120);fig(12,122,303,307,342)
sub('6.4 Benchmark C — Parallel Boolean dataflow: fault-model sensitivity')
add('The H100 laboratory v0.1 [51] extends Benchmark A to inversion as well as stuck-at-zero faults. Thirty matched seeded portfolios at each of K = 8, 16 and 32 outputs exhaust all 4,096 states of 12 Boolean inputs. Dedicated, shared, modular and buffered designs passed all 360 portfolio-level equivalence checks; 32,168 modeled fault cases were evaluated. Integrators and backup OR gates are explicitly faultable. Equal-site blast is the number of outputs changed on at least one input, averaged over sites within each design and over seeds. It is not an error rate or field failure probability. The related synthetic Boolean construction limits independence from Benchmark A.')
newtable('Table 7. Boolean-dataflow laboratory v0.1, K = 32. Mean modeled counts and output blast.', ['Design','Nodes','Motif stuck-zero','All-site stuck-zero','All-site inversion'],[['Dedicated','96.0','0.941','0.941','1.000'],['Shared','47.9','3.795','1.886','2.006'],['Modular','54.2','2.718','1.667','1.773'],['Buffered','89.6','0.108','1.008','2.395']], [120,70,102,100,100])
diagram('boolean','Figure 12. Fault-model dependence of protective architecture. Mean all-site output blast for the two named fault types; no hardware failure probabilities are inferred [51].')
add('At 32 outputs, the modeled saving from full sharing is about half the dedicated node count. The buffered design shows strong motif-only protection against stuck-zero faults (0.108 versus 0.941), but the all-site mean is 1.008 versus 0.941 for dedicated. Inversion changes the comparison: buffered mean disruption reaches 2.395 versus 1.000 for dedicated. The protection is therefore conditional on both the fault mechanism and the scope of the coordinator model. No actual GPU execution, energy or throughput was measured in this experiment.')
sub('6.5 Benchmark D — Parallel matrix workload: independence of protective dependencies')
add('The Hopper-inspired matrix pilot v0.2 [52] compares 30 seeded portfolios of 64 products of 8 × 8 integer matrices, in eight groups of eight outputs. Four designs allocate 16 staged A copies, 72 products and eight routers, with nominally two root copies; some roots are unused. This matches selected counts, not area, traffic, latency, energy, source acquisition or complete verification costs. Shared parity uses the primary staged A operand; separate-stage parity uses a standby from the same root; separate-root parity uses a separately injectable logical root; selective duplication recomputes one selected tile per group. Algorithm-based fault tolerance (ABFT) is established prior work [53].')
add('The pilot records 7,680 fault-free output checks and 33,000 modeled fault trials, with deterministic injections at named compute, group and upstream sites plus selected paired faults. Wrong outputs are present and differ from the test oracle; missing outputs are unavailable; unflagged wrong outputs lie outside flagged scope. The oracle is not used for recovery. A flag does not localize or correct an error. The model implements no fail-closed policy and does not assume independence of B operands. Independent injection into two logical roots is a modeled property, not evidence of independent physical sources.')
newtable('Table 8. Matrix pilot v0.2. Mean unflagged wrong outputs per named trial.', ['Scenario','Shared parity','Separate stage','Separate root','Selective duplicate'],[['Compute corruption','0','0','0','0.875'],['Staged primary corruption','8','0','0','7'],['Primary-root corruption','64','64','0','64'],['Both roots identically corrupt','64','64','64','64'],['Shared B corruption','1','1','1','1'],['Opposing errors','2','2','2','1'],['Primary corruption + check unavailable','8','8','8','8']], [175,77,80,78,82])
diagram('matrix','Figure 13. Mean incorrect outputs escaping detection per named modeled fault. Separate staging addresses local corruption, separate roots expose the nominated upstream fault, and identical corruption defeats all. Detection is not correction [52].')
add('All three parity designs recover a single known compute erasure. Two named erasures leave two missing for parity and one for selective duplication, a selection favoring the duplicate comparator. A router outage loses eight outputs for every design. Corrupting the separate verification root flags all 64 correct data outputs; a corrupted checksum flags correct outputs too. Identical root corruption, shared B corruption, opposing compute errors and absent checks reveal limits. Event-specific outcomes are not combined into expected reliability because event probabilities are unknown. Results illustrate checksum algebra and specified dependency topology, not a new ABFT method, physical H100 failure behavior or a demonstrated engineering gain.')
